  "record": {
    "read_status": "full",
    "file_problem": "none",
    "relation": "uses_without_test",
    "tranche": "T4",
    "tranche_reason": "The paper tests a different, iterative PEM unlearning method and reports target-task performance and retained-task utility. It does not test whether recovery attacks work.",
    "gate_note": "The paper reports unlearning results, but no fine-tuning recovery attack, activation-direction ablation, quantization attack, or prompting-only recovery test. Toxicity, NER, and coding results measure the unlearning outcomes and utility relevant to version A; they do not establish that knowledge was removed from the weights. Cited findings from other papers are not recorded as rows. The rows below capture the paper's final Ours results; stepwise trajectories and data-source/orthogonality ablations are not recovery tests.",
    "test_reason": "A new unlearning method (self-generated forget data plus iterative PEM add/subtract) is evaluated only on its own forget/retain trade-off against baselines. There is no fine-tuning, internal-edit, quantization or prompting recovery test. Version A's defining part, that simple attacks don't recover the target, is never probed, and no other version is touched. The targets (toxic generation, Person NER, coding skill) are behaviours or skills, not held-out factual knowledge. The study is a method paper reporting its own performance, not a test of shallow versus deep unlearning.",
    "authors_conclusion": "Correct. The authors conclude that self-generated forget data with iterative PEM unlearning gives effective targeted forgetting with minimal utility loss, across three tasks and two models. They do not evaluate recovery attacks or whether knowledge is removed from the weights.",
    "recipe": "different",
    "recipe_differences": "The paper proposes self-generated forget data and iterative PEM composition, rather than reproducing the cited unlearning methods or their recovery evaluations.",
    "method_sensitivity": "Task and model family affect the reported target and retained-task outcomes; the paper also reports different results across unlearning steps and subtraction thresholds.",
    "fits_no_version": [
      {
        "finding": "Iterative PEM unlearning with self-generated forget data cuts target behaviour sharply while retained-task scores mostly hold. On LLaMA3-8B, MBPP+ goes to 0.000 and Person F1 falls 0.537 to 0.143. Utility costs include GSM8K 0.744 to 0.651. Similar results hold on Mistral-7B. No recovery attack is run.",
        "proposed_claim": "Surface-level unlearning efficacy (forget/retain trade-off) of PEM-based methods, without robustness testing.",
